compemu_support_arm: a RAM block's interpreter warm-up happens once, then it is compiled

compile_block() gives a block in RAM an interpreter-dispatch first generation
(optlevel 0, "bi->count = 9"), meaning to escalate to native code "on the next
count expiry after 10 dispatches". The branch that sets that count tests only
`optlev == 0`, and a block that has run out its countdown is still at optlevel
0, so the same branch is taken again: another optlevel-0 generation, another 9,
for ever. The branch that escalates (`optlev < max_optlev`) is never reached
from level 0.

Measured on a Raspberry Pi 4 running Speedometer in System 7.6.1, in 187 s:
220,000 first compilations, 6.67 million recompilations after a countdown
expired, 6.3 million of them at optlevel 0 and 30,000 above it. A block of 3.5
instructions is rebuilt every ten executions and never becomes native code;
every rebuild emits a new stub into the cache, which was therefore full and
flushed every few seconds. Strict mode (B2_JIT_STRICT_FULL) does not pass
through this branch, which is why its figures, and the fork's, look as they do.

Warm up once: the interpreter generation is chosen only for a block that was
invalid, and a block that comes back with a countdown expired takes the
existing escalation.

